---
import CompliantLayout from '@/layouts/CompliantLayout.astro';
import PageIntro from '@/components/PageIntro.astro';
import home from '@/data/spawn-home.json';

const b = home.brand;
const support = (home as { supportEmail?: string }).supportEmail || 'hello@subaudit.org';
const associateTag = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_ASSOCIATE_TAG ?? 'subaudit-20';
const defaultAuthorSlug = (home as { defaultAuthorSlug?: string }).defaultAuthorSlug || 'morgan-hale-subaudit';
---

<CompliantLayout
  title={`How ${b.fullName} researches and reports`}
  description={`${b.fullName}'s methodology for annualizing subscription stacks, surfacing trial fine print, and finding swaps that lower monthly burn.`}
  sectionAds="legal"
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Methodology"
    title="How we audit stacks, annualize costs, and find the swap"
    description={`${b.fullName} exists because most subscription advice hides the annual total and the cancel path. Here is exactly how we work.`}
  />

  <section class="max-w-site mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-14 md:pb-20">
    <article class="content-panel max-w-prose mx-auto prose prose-lg prose-ink">
      <h2>Our mission</h2>
      <p>
        Add up every recurring charge on a card statement, annualize the real cost, and show
        readers where to cancel, downgrade, or swap before another year of quiet spend. The goal
        is specificity &mdash; generic &ldquo;cut your subscriptions&rdquo; lists are a dime a dozen.
      </p>

      <h2>What we track</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Streaming and channel add-ons (Prime Video, Max, Disney+, niche sports bundles).</li>
        <li>AI and productivity SaaS with monthly vs annual plan math.</li>
        <li>Amazon Subscribe &amp; Save, Audible, Kindle Unlimited, and digital bounty trials.</li>
        <li>Free-trial renewal dates, card-on-file requirements, and published cancel URLs.</li>
        <li>Household auto-ship and delivery subscriptions with per-unit annualized totals.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How we verify a stack</h2>
      <p>
        We don&rsquo;t publish a savings claim from a single screenshot. Every audit requires at
        least two of the following agreeing on the price, renewal terms, and cancel path:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Live checkout or account-billing pages captured on separate dates.</li>
        <li>Official plan pages and published commission or bounty documentation.</li>
        <li>Reader exports (redacted statements or app billing screens).</li>
        <li>Our own test sign-ups where terms allow cancellation without penalty.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When evidence is thin or a vendor changes pricing mid-review, we say so.
        &ldquo;Possibly rising&rdquo; is a different headline than &ldquo;$14.99/mo &times; 12 = $179.88/yr.&rdquo;
      </p>

      <h2>How we pick the alternative</h2>
      <p>
        Every audit ends with a specific swap &mdash; a cheaper annual plan, a bundled tier that
        replaces two services, a free tier that covers 80% of the use case, or a one-time purchase
        instead of another rental. The alternative must clear four bars:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Lower annualized cost</strong> after factoring in any up-front hardware or migration time.</li>
        <li><strong>Actually available</strong> in the regions our readers use &mdash; not a geo-locked promo.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest cancel path</strong> with a documented URL or in-app steps we tested or verified.</li>
        <li><strong>We&rsquo;d switch to it ourselves</strong>, and most of the time we already have.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No sponsored placements.</strong> We do not accept paid promotion from any
          service {b.fullName} covers. If that ever changes, the page carrying the promotion will say so plainly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Partner links are disclosed.</strong> Amazon links use our Associates tag
          (<strong>{associateTag}</strong>). SaaS picks require a published commission rate and cookie window before we recommend.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Named, dated, numbered.</strong> Every guide states the plan name, monthly and
          annual price, trial end date when relevant, and the specific cancel or downgrade steps.
          Soft claims don&rsquo;t ship.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Disclosure</h2>
      <p>
        {b.fullName} is funded by affiliate revenue from Amazon Associates and select SaaS
        programs. When you click through partner links and sign up or buy, we earn a commission at
        no extra cost to you. See the full <a href="/disclosure/">partner disclosure</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        {b.fullName} uses AI tools for research drafting and initial article structure.
        Every piece is reviewed and edited by{' '}
        <a href={`/authors/${defaultAuthorSlug}/`}>Morgan Hale</a>
        before publish. Dollar amounts, renewal dates, and cancel URLs are verified
        by a human before they appear on the site.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Wrong price? Wrong renewal date? Link to the billing page or statement export and email
        <a href={`mailto:${support}`}>{support}</a>. We correct fast
        and leave a visible &ldquo;corrected on&rdquo; stamp on the page so readers know
        what changed and when.
      </p>

      <h2>What {b.fullName} is not</h2>
      <p>
        {b.fullName} is consumer subscription journalism &mdash; not financial advice, not
        tax guidance, not a substitute for reading your own card agreement. If recurring charges
        are putting real stress on your monthly budget, we&rsquo;ll point you to the swap, but we
        can&rsquo;t tell you how much to allocate to discretionary spend.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</CompliantLayout>
